<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Albert Suerbeer</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1200–1273</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Albert Suerbeer was a German churchman who became the first archbishop of Riga. Earlier archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armagh">Armagh</a> in Ireland and papal legate for the Baltic lands and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, he took up the see of Riga, raised to an archbishopric in 1255, with authority over the bishoprics of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonia">Livonia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>. His rule was marked by disputes with the Livonian Order and the city of Riga.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/albert-suerbeer.jpg" alt="Albert Suerbeer" width="320" height="426" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: SVG created by the uploader · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Archbishopric_of_Riga.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
